<h2 id="techapp" class="tex-section"><span data-tag="A3B2D6">8.3</span> Simplicial Categories</h2>

<p>
Among the many different models for higher category theory, the theory of simplicial categories is perhaps the most rigid. This can be either a curse or a blessing, depending on the situation. For the most part, we have chosen to use the less rigid theory of $\infty $-categories (see §<a href="/tag/95994B" data-tag="95994B">1.1.2</a>) throughout this book. However, some arguments are substantially easier to carry out in the setting of simplicial categories. For this reason, we have devoted the final section of this appendix to giving a review of the theory of simplicial categories. 
</p>
<p>
There exists a model structure on the category $\sCat $ of (small) simplicial categories, which was constructed by Bergner (<span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/bergner">bergner</a>]</span>). In §<a href="/tag/F81061" data-tag="F81061">8.3.2</a>, we will describe an analogous model structure on the category $\SCat $ of $\mathbf{S}$-enriched categories, where $\mathbf{S}$ is a suitable model category. To formulate this generalization, we will need to employ the language of monoidal model categories, which we review in §<a href="/tag/86E375" data-tag="86E375">8.3.1</a>. Under mild assumptions on $\mathbf{S}$, one can show that a $\mathbf{S}$-enriched category $\calC $ is fibrant if and only if each of the mapping objects $\bHom _{\calC }(X,Y)$ is a fibrant object of $\mathbf{S}$.CATsubS@$\SCat $ 
</p>
<p>
In §<a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a>, we will study the category $\mathbf{A}^{\calC }$ of diagrams $\calC \rightarrow \mathbf{A}$, where $\calC $ is a small category and $\mathbf{A}$ a model category, both enriched over some fixed model category $\mathbf{S}$. In the enriched setting we can again endow $\mathbf{A}^{\calC }$ with projective and injective model structures, which can be used to define homotopy limits and colimits. 
</p>
<p>
Putting aside set-theoretic technicalities, every $\mathbf{S}$-enriched model category $\mathbf{A}$ gives rise to a fibrant object of $\SCat $: namely, the full subcategory $\mathbf{A}^{\circ } \subseteq \mathbf{A}$ spanned by the fibrant-cofibrant objects. In §<a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a>, we will introduce a path object for $\mathbf{A}^{\circ }$, which will enable us to perform some calculations in the homotopy category of $\SCat $. 
</p>
<p>
In §<a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a>, we will consider the problem of constructing homotopy colimits in the category $\SCat $ of $\mathbf{S}$-enriched categories. Our main result, Theorem <a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a>, asserts that the formation of homotopy colimits in $\SCat $ is compatible with the formation of (tensor) products in $\SCat $. We will apply this result in §<a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a> to study the homotopy theory of internal mapping objects in $\SCat $. 
</p>
<p>
We conclude this section with §<a href="/tag/" data-tag="">None</a>, where we discuss localizations of (simplicial) model categories. 
</p>
